---
import ToolPage from '../../components/ToolPage.astro';
import ContentTable from '../../components/ContentTable.astro';
import { YEAR } from '../../data/site-config';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { rel, srcs } from '../../lib/links';
import { pensionImposable, impotBareme, P } from '../../lib/engine/pension';
import { formatMoney } from '../../lib/format';
const lang = 'en';
const $ = (x: number) => formatMoney(x, 0, lang);
const F = P.fiscalite;
const ex = pensionImposable(3600);
const faqs = [
  { q: 'Do you have to declare child support received in France?', a: 'Yes. Support received for a child is taxable income for the household the child lives in, declared under pensions, on the line for maintenance received (“pensions alimentaires perçues”). Service-public.fr notes that these amounts never appear on the pre-filled return: you must add them yourself, because the tax office does not know them in advance.' },
  { q: 'What allowance applies to child support received?', a: `The tax office automatically applies a 10% allowance to the household’s total declared pensions. For ${F.revenus} income it is at least ${$(F.abattement_min_par_pensionne)} per recipient, without exceeding the pension itself, and at most ${$(F.abattement_max_par_foyer)} per tax household, all pensions combined, retirement pensions included.` },
  { q: 'Does an adult child declare the support a parent pays them?', a: `Yes, if they file their own return: they declare support only up to the amount the parent was able to deduct, for example at most ${$(F.plafond_majeur)} for an adult child. Any surplus is not declared. If they are attached to one parent’s tax household, that parent declares the support paid by the other.` },
];
---
<ToolPage lang={lang} id="imposable" kind="imposable" breadcrumbLabel="Is it taxable?"
  title={`Child support taxable in France ${YEAR}? Declaring it`}
  h1="Is child support received taxable in France? What the receiving parent declares"
  description={`Is child support taxable in France in ${YEAR}? Declaring it on the right line, the 10% allowance (minimum €454, maximum €4,439) and the estimated extra tax.`}
  intro="What one parent deducts, the other declares: support moves tax household along with the money."
  resume={`Child support received for a child is taxable in France for the parent who receives it: it is added to their income under pensions. It never appears on the pre-filled tax return, so you must enter it yourself. The tax office applies a 10% allowance to all the household’s pensions, with a minimum of ${$(F.abattement_min_par_pensionne)} per recipient and a maximum of ${$(F.abattement_max_par_foyer)} per household for ${F.revenus} income. Example: ${$(300)} a month, ${$(3600)} a year, gets an allowance of ${$(ex.abattement)}, so ${$(ex.imposable)} is added to taxable income. The real effect depends on the household’s number of tax shares, often increased by the children themselves: for many single parents it stays small or nil. Parents used to systems where child support is tax-free on both sides, as in some English-speaking countries, should note the difference. The calculator estimates the extra tax with the ${YEAR} scale.`}
  faqs={faqs} related={rel(['deduction', 'majeur', 'asf', 'home', 'epoux', 'caf'], lang)} sources={srcs(['f35777', 'fiscalite', 'f1419'], lang)}>
  <h2>Allowance and taxable amount by support received</h2>
  <ContentTable headers={['Support per month', 'Per year', 'Allowance', 'Added to taxable income']} align={['r', 'r', 'r', 'r']} rows={[100, 200, 300, 500, 800, 1200].map((p) => { const x = pensionImposable(p * 12); return [$(p), $(p * 12), $(x.abattement), $(x.imposable)]; })} />
  <p>For small amounts the {$(F.abattement_min_par_pensionne)} minimum matters more than the 10%: support of {$(100)} a month only adds {$(pensionImposable(1200).imposable)} to taxable income.</p>
  <h2>Estimated extra tax, single parent with two shares</h2>
  <ContentTable caption="2025 income scale, without décote or cap" headers={['Other taxable income', 'Support €200/month', '€400/month']} align={['l', 'r', 'r']} rows={[18000, 30000, 45000].map((i) => [$(i), ...[200, 400].map((p) => $(impotBareme(i + pensionImposable(p * 12).imposable, 2) - impotBareme(i, 2)))])} />
  <p>These estimates leave out the décote and the extra share specific to single parents; they give an order of magnitude. The mirror rule, for the paying parent, is on <a href={route('deduction', lang)}>tax deduction</a>.</p>
</ToolPage>
